class:: Vibrato2
summary:: Measure vibrato rate and extent from a supplied pitch contour
categories:: UGens>Analysis>Pitch
related:: Guides/MIRa, Classes/Pitch2, Classes/FundamentalCandidates, Classes/PitchStability, Classes/PitchDeviation

description::
An original least-squares descriptor, operating on a supplied pitch contour without duplicating F0 estimation. It samples the cents trajectory at approximately 100 Hz, removes a confidence-weighted straight-line trend, and fits sine/cosine pairs on a 0.05 Hz rate grid. The best fit defines rate and twice its amplitude defines extent. Presence requires confidence at least 0.6, sufficient extent and two cycles of the slowest allowed rate. Sampling is intended for smooth pitch contours; modulation above the contour Nyquist rate can alias. Missing data and very short notes inhibit detection. This sinusoid model handles regular vibrato; irregular modulation may have low confidence.

Vanilla link::Classes/Vibrato:: generates vibrato. Vibrato2 measures the modulation already present in a frequency control signal.

classmethods::
method:: kr
argument:: freq
Supplied control-rate frequency in Hz; zero means unvoiced.

argument:: pitchConfidence
Observation weight from 0 through 1. At least 80% weighted coverage is needed.

argument:: minRate
Lowest modulation frequency in Hz, at least 0.5. At least two periods at this rate must fit in the window.

argument:: maxRate
Highest modulation frequency in Hz; internally limited below the decimated contour Nyquist frequency.

argument:: minExtent
Minimum peak-to-peak extent in cents for present = 1.

argument:: windowTime
Literal analysis window from 0.1 through 5 seconds. First result waits for the full window; estimates update approximately 20 times per second.

returns::
An Array: presence flag, modulation rate in Hz, peak-to-peak extent in cents, explained residual-variance fraction times weighted coverage.

examples::
Vibrato2 measures the instantaneous modulation rate (Hz) and peak-to-peak extent (cents) of pitch vibrato extracted from a continuous pitch contour. In this example, detected vibrato parameters synchronize an amplitude tremolo effect: whenever the performer introduces vibrato, the sound tremolos synchronously with the acoustic rate. An interactive code::MIRaGUI.scope:: tracks the detected vibrato rate in Hz.
code::
(
s.waitForBoot({
    var path = MIRaGUI.soundPath("melody.wav");
    ~miraBuffer = if(path.notNil) { Buffer.read(s, path) } { nil };
    ~miraBus = Bus.control(s, 1);
    s.sync;
    ~miraExample = {
        var source = if(~miraBuffer.notNil) {
            PlayBuf.ar(1, ~miraBuffer, BufRateScale.kr(~miraBuffer), loop: 1)
        } {
            // Synthetic melody fallback
            Saw.ar(Select.kr(LFNoise0.kr(2).range(0, 5), [220, 261.63, 293.66, 329.63, 392, 440])) * 0.2
        };
        // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);

        var pitch = PitchMPM.kr(source, 80, 1200, 2048, 0.25);
        var result = Vibrato2.kr(pitch[0], pitch[1], minRate: 3, maxRate: 9, minExtent: 15, windowTime: 0.6);
        var presence = result[0];
        var rate = result[1];
        var extent = result[2];

        // Amplitude tremolo synchronized with detected vibrato rate
        var tremoloLFO = SinOsc.kr(rate.clip(2, 10)).range(1.0 - (presence.lag(0.05) * 0.6), 1.0);
        var sound = (source * tremoloLFO * 0.3) ! 2;

        if(~miraBus.notNil) { Out.kr(~miraBus, rate) };
        sound * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.2)
    }.play;

    ~miraGUI = MIRaGUI.scope("Vibrato Rate", ~miraBus, 0.0, 10.0, "Hz");
});
)
::
code::
(
~miraExample.free;
if(~miraGUI.notNil) { ~miraGUI.close };
if(~miraBus.notNil) { ~miraBus.free };
if(~miraBuffer.notNil) { ~miraBuffer.free };
)
::
